- Retrieved practices:
- Out-of-bounds pointers and array subscripts
WHAT: Forming or using pointers or array subscripts that are outside the bounds of the target array (including negative indices, indices ≥ length, or pointers past flexible array members) causes undefined behavior and may access invalid memory.
WHY: Out-of-bounds access can lead to crashes, data corruption, information disclosure, or exploitable memory-safety vulnerabilities.
HOW: Validate all indices before use (index >= 0 and index < array_length); never perform pointer arithmetic that moves a pointer outside the array (except one-past-the-end without dereference); do not generate or dereference pointers past flexible array members when no elements are present.
EXAMPLE (C, before): `int f(int *a, size_t len, int i){ return a[i]; } /* i may be negative or ≥ len */`
EXAMPLE (C, after): `int f(int *a, size_t len, int i){ if (i < 0 || (size_t)i >= len) return ERR; return a[i]; }`

- Do not form or use out-of-bounds pointers or array subscripts
WHAT: Forming pointers or array indices that are outside the bounds of an array (including negative indices) and then dereferencing them.
WHY: Out-of-bounds access can read or modify unrelated memory, leading to information disclosure, data corruption, or crashes.
HOW: Always validate indices before use (check lower and upper bounds), or use unsigned index types and ensure `index < ARRAY_SIZE` before doing pointer arithmetic or subscripting.
EXAMPLE (C): Before: `int *p = table + index;` with only `if (index < TABLESIZE)`. After: `if (index >= 0 && index < TABLESIZE) { int *p = table + index; }`

- CWE-122/CWE-129: Out-of-bounds array access and improper index validation
WHAT: Code forms or uses out-of-bounds pointers/array subscripts when resizing arrays (for example, using `if (size < pos)` and writing `table[pos]` without safe bounds and overflow checks).
WHY: Out-of-bounds writes can corrupt heap memory, cause crashes, or enable code execution (heap-based buffer overflows) due to invalid array index use.
HOW: Always validate that the index is within bounds using `<=`, check for integer overflow before computing new buffer sizes, call `realloc` with the validated size, and update global size variables only after `realloc` succeeds.
EXAMPLE (C):
Before: `if (size < pos) { size = pos + 1; table = realloc(table, sizeof(*table)*size); } table[pos] = value;`
After: `if (size <= pos) { if ((SIZE_MAX - 1 < pos) || (pos + 1 > SIZE_MAX/sizeof(*table))) return -1; tmp = realloc(table, sizeof(*table)*(pos+1)); if (!tmp) return -1; table = tmp; size = pos + 1; } table[pos] = value;`



